  "speaker": "Abhinavagupta (commentator voice)",
  "question": "The opening of the IPVV has made the prostration to Śiva. Now Abhinavagupta must\nexplain what that prostration actually is and means — and who is eligible to make it. The commentary\non the word \"kathaṃcit āsādya\" (having attained, in some way) opens up: the attaining of the Lord is\nthe attainment of *dāsya* (the servant-ness), and the servant-ness is the very heart of the eligibility\nand the devotion. The whole is framed against the Gītā's account of the four kinds of devotees.",
  "premises": [
   "The right teacher (samyagvyutpādakaḥ śāstrakāraḥ) is he who does not abandon the view; the Buddhists and\nthe rest are its makers-and-haters (this is set out in the Kathāmukhatilaka). The ṭīkā-kāra, seeing an\nun-precedented form of the prostration, makes the \"jaya-namaskāra\" and the rest, for the ones who\nsupport it (the ādi includes the vandana, the pradhyāna). The form that rests in the two [the Lord and\nthe devotee] must, being conveyed through one of them, culminate in the rest of both: \"the jar shines,\nthe jar sees\" — the jar, being manifested, manifests [its own manifestation] — this is the\nobject-understanding. The prostration is two-resting. The \"jaya/jayasi/jayeti\" words, though moving by\nthe param-eśvara's excess, must have the self-subordinating as the support (else the excess fails); the\n\"namaḥ/namāmi/vande\" words, though moving by the self-subordinating re-recollection, must rest in the\nLord's excess. The two are one thing, two-aspect, made manifest by the word for the whole audience.",
   "\"Kathaṃcit\" (in some way) says that the dāsya is extremely hard to get — so the more is the\nself-subordination stated. \"Mahāphala\" (great-fruit): the Lord's excess is attained, being the servant,\nin the Lord; the \"great-fruit\" is the cause of the attainment of all the fortunes (samastasampat) — the\nword is the pratyabhijñā's distinction and the dāsya's distinction (the Vṛtti: \"dāsyalakṣmī\" — the\nlakṣmīs born of the dāsya, all the fortunes, attained from the param-eśvara's grace; else the\nlakṣmī-word would be meaningless). \"Vācāpi vā\" (even by speech): by the 'api', also by the body. The\nprostration and the excess, resting in the mental experience (the mind-awareness) and the word-essence,\nnot in the inert body; so the devotee, offering the undivided mind-speech (the consciousness-power),\noffers the whole world to the Lord. (The āgama: \"and he should offer his own self.\")",
   "The prostration by the word is the upacāra (the metaphor), the mark by which the mind-offering is\ninferred; not the primary [sense]. The \"satyam\" (true): the removal of obstacles is indeed attained,\nbut the greater purpose is that of the verse-fragment bound to the prostration. The \"adyāpi\" (even now):\nun-brokenly, by the entry into the book, the whole heart is crossed; the offering is in that, not in the\nself. The \"vyākhyātṛ\" (the explainer) is honored; in the pratyabhijñā's establishment, the Lord's\nfavor is inferred, preceded by the Lord's favor. The objection: \"the eligibility suits the explainer,\nbut how the listeners?\" — the listener does not establish the recognition, nor the dāsya, apart; the\nre-recollection is one. [The four Gītā-bhaktas — the distressed, the seeker of knowledge, the seeker of\nwealth, the knower — are all eligible, in their gradations, for the dāsya and the recognition.]"
  ],
  "inference": "Decision for L2\nProceed. The argument is understood: the prostration is the two-resting homage — self-subordinating and\nLord-exalting, made in the mind and speech, not the inert body; the dāsya (servant-ness) is the heart\nof the eligibility, attained by the Lord's grace and fruitful of all fortunes; and the devotees — the\nfour Gītā-kinds — are all eligible, in their gradations, for the recognition.",
  "uncertainties": [
   "The \"kathāmukhatilake\" (in the Kathāmukhatilaka) reference — a lost or separate work; cited, the",
   "The exact four Gītā-devotees' gradation in the eligibility (the text's continuation) — kept at the"
  ],
